# Selection on observables: matching, regression, and the leap of faith
subtitle: "When people who look alike on paper can stand in for a lottery: what matching and regression each assume, and where both fail together."
tags: ["selection on observables","matching","propensity score","regression","health insurance"]
teaches: ["methods/selection-on-observables","methods/matching","methods/propensity-score","concepts/common-support"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Arrows or potential outcomes? Bad controls and colliders","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/arrows-or-potential-outcomes-bad-controls-and-colliders.cast"}

## Twins on paper
voice: female
level: advanced

