    card title "Twins on paper"

Without a lottery, can we still get a lottery's answer, by comparing insured and uninsured people who look the same on paper?
    draw h_ins h_un h_gap rule_h

Picture three hundred adults on low incomes, and no lottery anywh (@highlight h_ins h_un@)ere. The numbers are made up, to keep the idea plain.
    draw all_lab rule_a

Two hundred of them have coverage. In the last thirty days they had fourteen good days of (@draw all_d1@) physical health, on average.
    draw all_n1

The hundred without covera (@draw all_d0@)ge had twenty-two.
    draw all_n0

Taken at face value, insurance  (@draw all_g@)costs eight good days a month.
    point at.ref all_d1 gesture underline

Nobody believes that. Coverage is something people sign up for, and the sick have the most reason to.
    highlight all_g effect circle

So sort them first, by what the records show: how many chronic conditions each person has on f (@draw h_x@)ile. Call that X.
    draw h_x2

With none on file, twenty have signed up and eighty haven't.
    draw r0_x r0_n1 r0_n0

With one condition it flips: eighty signed up, twenty didn't.
    draw r1_x r1_n1 r1_n0

And with two or more, all hundred are covered.
    draw r2_x r2_n1 r2_n0 r2_d0

    quiz "Inside the row with no conditions on file, will the insured still look worse off than the uninsured?"
        intro: Before the numbers, a guess.
        right: Better off: inside one row the sickness on file is the same for both sides, so it no longer drags the insured down.
        wrong: In this row nobody has a condition on file. What was it that made the insured look sicker overall?
        * Yes, worse off
        * About the same
        + No, better off

Inside that row, the insured had twenty-six good days, and the uninsured twenty-four.
    draw r0_d1 r0_d0

Among those with one condition, sixteen against fourteen.
    draw r1_d1 r1_d0

Both rows tell the same story: coverage goes with two more good days, not eight fewer.
    draw r0_g r1_g

The minus eight was never the insurance. It was the sick sorting themselves into coverage, and the rows have sorted them back.
    highlight all_g r0_g r1_g

Comparing only people the records cannot tell apart is called matching.
    highlight r0_x r1_x effect box

The bottom row has ten good days among the covered, and no one unin (@point at.ref r2_d0 gesture circle@)sured to compare them with. Hold on t (@highlight r2_g effect circle@)o that empty cell.
    draw r2_d1 r2_g

But is plu (@highlight r0_g r1_g effect circle@)s two the effect of coverage? Only if one thing  (@highlight r0_x r1_x r2_x@)is true of the people inside each row.

## What the bar asserts
voice: female
level: advanced

    math f1 tex "E[{Y_i(0)} \\mid D_i=1, X_i] = E[{Y_i(0)} \\mid D_i=0, X_i]" size 56 colors {"{Y_i(1)}":"#2f6b8f","{Y_i(0)}":"#b5482e"} x 570 y 685 hidden true
    text row_title "One condition" x 200 y 490 font_size 40 hidden true
    text dnote "D = 1: covered" #8f887c x 570 y 760 font_size 36 hidden true
    math h_y1 tex "{Y_i(1)}" size 52 colors {"{Y_i(1)}":"#2f6b8f"} x 560 y 490 hidden true
    math h_y0 tex "{Y_i(0)}" size 52 colors {"{Y_i(0)}":"#b5482e"} x 800 y 490 hidden true
    text h_g "Gain" #8a5fa8 x 1010 y 490 font_size 46 hidden true
    path rule thick #3d3833 points [[50,450],[1080,450]] hidden true
    text ins_lab "80 insured" #2f6b8f x 200 y 360 font_size 46 hidden true
    text ins_y1 "16" #2f6b8f x 560 y 360 font_size 70 hidden true
    text ins_q "?" #8f887c x 800 y 360 font_size 74 hidden true
    text ins_b14 "14" #b5482e x 800 y 360 font_size 70 style.opacity 0.55 hidden true
    text ins_12 "12" #b5482e x 800 y 360 font_size 70 style.italic true hidden true
    text ins_g2 "2" #8a5fa8 x 1010 y 360 font_size 70 hidden true
    text ins_g4 "4" #8a5fa8 x 1010 y 360 font_size 74 hidden true
    text un_lab "20 uninsured" #b5482e x 200 y 220 font_size 46 hidden true
    text un_q "?" #8f887c x 560 y 220 font_size 74 hidden true
    text un_y0 "14" #b5482e x 800 y 220 font_size 70 hidden true
    arrow borrow thick #8f887c from.x 860 from.y 235 to.x 860 to.y 350 hidden true
    math f_lot tex "({Y_i(1)}, {Y_i(0)}) \\perp D_i" size 56 colors {"{Y_i(1)}":"#2f6b8f","{Y_i(0)}":"#b5482e"} x 570 y 595 hidden true
    text why_un "lost a form?" #8f887c x 200 y 165 font_size 36 style.italic true hidden true
    text why_in "felt unwell?" #8f887c x 200 y 305 font_size 36 style.italic true hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["f1","row_title","dnote","h_y1","h_y0","h_g","rule","ins_lab","ins_y1","ins_q","ins_b14","ins_12","ins_g2","ins_g4","un_lab","un_q","un_y0","borrow","f_lot","why_un","why_in"] hidden true

    card title "What the bar asserts"

What does matching assume, when it calls that plus two an effect? Look inside one row: people with one condition on file.
    draw row_title h_y1 h_y0 h_g rule

Eighty of them are insured, and we see their sixteen  (@draw ins_y1@)good days, their life with coverage.
    draw ins_lab

Their life without coverage never happened. It is a blank.
    draw ins_q

For the twenty uninsured it's the other way round: we see fourteen, and t (@draw un_q@)heir covered life is the blank.
    draw un_lab un_y0

Matching fills the insured's blank with  (@hide ins_q@)thei (@draw ins_b14@)r neighbours' fourteen.
    draw borrow

That is where plus two comes (@highlight ins_y1 ins_b14@) from: sixteen, minus a borrowed fourteen.
    draw ins_g2

The borrowing is the whole assumption. Write (@draw dnote@) D for coverage, one for covered: had they stayed uninsured, the insured would have done just as well as the uninsured beside them.
    draw f1

Economists ask a little more, and write it with a bar: once you know X, coverage says nothing about either potential futur (@highlight h_y1 h_y0 effect underline@)e. They call it conditional independence.
    morph f1 tex "({Y_i(1)}, {Y_i(0)}) \\perp D_i \\mid X_i"

A lottery hands you the same line without the bar, because a draw cannot see anyone's health.
    draw f_lot

The bar is the leap of faith. It says that everything that sent people into coverage is written in the row label.
    highlight f1 part "\\mid X_i"

So what must be (@erase f_lot@) true of the twenty uninsured? That they stayed out for reasons unrelated to (@draw why_un@) their health: a lost form, a clinic that never mentioned it.

Now suppose many of the eighty signed up because they'd felt un (@highlight ins_lab effect underline@)well for months, and no doctor had written it down.
    draw why_in

Then their blank isn't fourteen. On average (@draw ins_12@), say it's twelve.
    erase ins_b14 borrow

Their true gain is s (@draw ins_g4@)ixteen minus twelve: four good days, not two.
    hide ins_g2

And nothing in the data warns you. The numbers you can see are exactly the same either way.
    highlight ins_y1 un_y0 effect circle

That is what conditional independence asserts: not something about your records, but about the people your records cannot tell apart.
    highlight f1

## No one to match
voice: female
level: advanced
sources:
    - id: rosenbaum1983
      title: The central role of the propensity score in observational studies for causal effects
      authors: Rosenbaum and Rubin, Biometrika
      year: 1983
      finding: Matching on the propensity score balances the covariates.
    - id: crump2009
      title: Dealing with limited overlap in estimation of average treatment effects
      authors: Crump, Hotz, Imbens and Mitnik, Biometrika
      year: 2009
      finding: 'Rule of thumb: keep propensity scores between 0.1 and 0.9.'

