    text b2_n "100" #2f6b8f x 915 y 390 font_size 46 hidden true
    math b2_p cites ["rosenbaum1983"] tex "p = 1" size 46 x 915 y 205 hidden true
    math cs cites ["crump2009"] tex "0 < p(X_i) < 1" size 56 x 562 y 680 hidden true
    text x2 "dropped" #b5482e x 915 y 300 font_size 46 hidden true
    path brk #8a5fa8 points [[60,150],[60,130],[712,130],[712,150]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text brk_t "the estimate: 200 people" #8a5fa8 x 386 y 85 font_size 40 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["b0_ins","b0_un","b0_box","b0_lab","b0_n","b0_nu","b0_p","b1_ins","b1_un","b1_box","b1_lab","b1_n","b1_nu","b1_p","b2_ins","b2_box","b2_lab","b2_n","b2_p","cs","x2","brk","brk_t"] hidden true

    card title "No one to match"

Back to the row where everyone is covered. What happens to people who have no one to be compared with?
    draw b0_box b1_box b2_box b0_lab b1_lab b2_lab

Shade each row by who has coverage: a fifth of the first row,
    draw b0_ins b0_un b0_n b0_nu

four fifths of the second,
    draw b1_ins b1_un b1_n b1_nu

and every single person in the third.
    draw b2_ins b2_n

That share is the propensity score: the chance of coverage, given what's on file.
    draw b0_p b1_p b2_p parallel true

Rosenbaum and Rubin showed in 1983 that people with the same score are balanced on everything on file, so one number can stand in for a long list.
    highlight b0_p b1_p b2_p

But a comparison needs both kinds of person at each score, so the score must lie strictly between zero and one. That is common support.
    draw cs

That is for the effect on everyone. For the effect on the insured, the score need only stay below one, and the (@point at.ref b2_p gesture circle@) third row fails even that.
    highlight cs

With a score of one, there is no uninsured neighbour to borrow from, so the blank can't be filled, only guessed.
    highlight b2_box effect box

So matching d (@draw x2@)rops the row.
    fade b2_ins b2_box b2_n b2_p to 0.3

    quiz "With the third row dropped, whose effect does the plus two now describe?"
        right: The first two rows: the hundred people in the third row have left the question, not just the data.
        wrong: Look at the faded row. Who is still in the comparison?
        * All three hundred adults
        + The two hundred in the first two rows
        * Everyone who has coverage

Plus two now speaks for two hundred people, not the three hundred we set out to study.
    draw brk brk_t

Ask about the people who have coverage, and it's worse: half of the two hundred insured sat in the dropped row.
    highlight b2_ins effect box

They're the sickest, where coverage might matter most, and the estimate is silent about them.
    highlight b2_lab effect underline

Nothing in the output flags it. The number arrives with a tidy standard error, for a smaller group than the one you meant.
    highlight brk_t

Real scores rarely hit zero or one exactly, so a common rule, from Crump, Hotz, Imbens and Mitnik, keeps only scores bet (@highlight b0_p b1_p effect circle@)ween point one and point nine.
    morph cs tex "0.1 < p(X_i) < 0.9"

Trimming is honest about what the data can say. But every person it drops changes the question, so say whom the answer is about.
    highlight brk brk_t

