    card title "Matching or regression?"

Matching is one way to fill the blanks; regression is another, and it comes in more than one kind. Put the rows on a chart to see.
    draw ax_x ax_y tk0 tk10 tk20 tl0 tl10 tl20 y_title xl0 xl1 xl2

The insured, in blue: twenty-six good days, sixteen, and ten, as conditions pile up.
    draw i0 il0 i1 il1 i2 il2

The uninsured, in red: twenty-four and fourteen, and nobody at all with two or more.
    draw u0 ul0 u1 ul1

Matching compares straight up, insid (@draw g1 gl1@)e each row: plus two, and plus two.
    draw g0 gl0

At two or more conditions it stops. (@highlight i2 effect circle@) There is no one to compare with, so it says nothing.
    draw q2

One kind of regression doesn't stop. Fit a li (@draw reg reg_t@)ne to the uninsured, and use it to predict their blanks.
    erase q2

At two or more conditions, the line predicts four good days for the uninsured.
    draw u2 ul2

So coverage would be worth six there: a number read off a ruler, not off people.
    draw g2 gl2

If the sickest would have done better or worse than a straight line, six is wrong, and nothing in the data can say so. That is where matching beats a prediction: it won't invent a blank with no neighbours.
    highlight reg

The regression most people run is a different t (@draw fr@)hing: good days on coverage, with a dummy for each row as the control.
    erase reg reg_t u2 ul2 g2 gl2

It weighs each row by its size times p times one minus p: sixteen, sixteen, and zero for the row where everyone is covered.
    draw wr_lab wr0 wr1 wr2

So it lands on plus two, and quietly drops the full-coverage row, just as matching does.
    highlight wr2 effect circle

Matching for the insured weighs the rows by how many insured they hold: twenty and eighty.
    draw wa_lab wa0 wa1 wa2

Here the gain is two in every row, so the weights don't matter. When the gain differs from row to row, they move the number, often only a little.
    highlight gl0 gl1 effect circle

Put the conditions in as one straight line instead, and regression says about one point two, because a straight lin (@draw lin_t@)e fits the insured badly too.
    morph fr tex "Y_i = \\alpha + \\tau D_i + \\beta X_i"

Regression has real gifts: it uses everyone, handles dozens of controls at once, and gives a standard error almost for free. But look at what every version used as the insured's life without coverage: the same red dots.
    highlight u0 u1 effect circle

If those dots are the wrong stand-in, matching and every regression are wrong together, and in the same direction.
    point at.ref u1 gesture tap

## When the stand-in is wrong
voice: female
level: advanced
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - y = 2 + x
      - y = 2 + 0*x
    params:
      h: {value: 0, min: 0, max: 4, label: hidden gap}
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 4
    y_range:
      - 0
      - 7
    show_equation: false
    box: full
    x_label: hidden gap (days)
    y_label: effect (good days)
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: h, curve: 0, label: false, guides: false, id: you}
adjust: {"x_label":{"scale":1.4},"y_label":{"scale":1.4}}
sources:
    - id: finkelstein2011
      title: 'The Oregon Health Insurance Experiment: Evidence from the First Year (NBER Working Paper 17190; QJE 2012)'
      authors: Finkelstein et al. (Oregon Health Study Group)
      year: 2011
      finding: 'Lottery: +1.3 good days (SE 0.56); surveys: -2.3, with controls -0.9.'
takeaways:
    - Matching compares within the records
    - No overlap changes the question
    - Both need the same leap of faith

    text yt0 "0" #8f887c at.data [-0.17,0] font_size 36 hidden true
    text yt2 "2" #8f887c at.data [-0.17,2] font_size 36 hidden true
    text yt4 "4" #8f887c at.data [-0.17,4] font_size 36 hidden true
    text yt6 "6" #8f887c at.data [-0.17,6] font_size 36 hidden true
    text xt1 "1" #8f887c at.data [1,-0.45] font_size 36 hidden true
    text xt2 "2" #8f887c at.data [2,-0.45] font_size 36 hidden true
    text takeaway_1 "Matching compares within the records" x 825 y 520 width 330 font_size 38 hidden true
    text takeaway_2 "No overlap changes the question" x 825 y 390 width 330 font_size 38 hidden true
    text takeaway_3 "Both need the same leap of faith" x 825 y 260 width 330 font_size 38 hidden true
    text lab_est "matching = regression" #b5482e at.data [2.9,1.35] font_size 40 hidden true
    text lab_truth "true effect" #2f6b8f at.data [2,5.3] font_size 42 hidden true
    scale oregon at.percent [50,42] cites ["finkelstein2011"] width 960 font_size 34 label "Oregon 2008: good days of physical health a month" min -4 max 4 value 1.3 markers [{"value":-2.3,"label":"surveys −2.3","color":"#b5482e"},{"value":-0.9,"label":"controls −0.9","color":"#b5482e"}] hidden true

    card title "When the stand-in is wrong"

How wr (@hide x_ticks y_ticks@)ong can that stand-in be? Across: how much sicker  (@highlight x_label@)the insured are than their uninsured twins, in ways no record shows.
    draw axes yt0 yt2 yt4 yt6 xt1 xt2

Matching, and a regression with a dummy for each row, both read plus two, whatever tha (@highlight curve_1@)t hidden gap is, because the data they see do not change.
    draw curve_1 lab_est

The true effect is plus two onl (@draw you@)y where the gap is zero. It climbs  (@highlight curve_0@)one day for every day of hidden gap.
    draw curve_0 lab_truth

Move the point to the right, toward insured people who were secretly sicker, and watch which line follows it.
    explore params ["h"] watch "Watch the point move toward insured people who were secretly sicker, and see which line follows it."

The truth climbs away, while both estimates sit still: neither method can see it move.
    animate params.h.value 2 duration 3

At a hidden gap of two, the case of the eighty in the one-condition row, on average, coverage  (@highlight curve_1@)is worth four, and both methods say two.
    point at.ref you gesture circle

Does this happen with real people? Oregon offers a check.
    hide axes x_label y_label yt0 yt2 yt4 yt6 xt1 xt2 curve_0 curve_1 lab_est lab_truth you

In two thousand and eight, Oregon held a  (@draw oregon@)lottery for Medicaid: about ninety thousand low-income adults signed up, and about thirty thousand names were drawn. Chance, not health, decided who got the offer.

Its researchers set their result beside national surveys of adults below the poverty line, which ask the same question: on how many of the last thirty days was your physical health good?
    highlight oregon

In those surveys, the insured reported about two and a quarter good days fewer than the uninsured.
    highlight oregon_marker_1 effect circle

Control for age, sex, race, schooling, marriage, children, work and income, and the gap shrinks to about one day fewer.
    highlight oregon_marker_2 effect circle

    ask "Where did Oregon's lottery put the effect of Medicaid on the same measure? Click the line." on oregon store g
        right: Close: you said {g:1}, and the lottery found {g.true:1} more good days a month.
        wrong: You said {g:1}. The lottery found {g.true:1}: coverage added good days.

Plus one and a third, give or take about one point one. The controls moved the estimate the right way, but never across zero.
    highlight oregon_answer

These are different people in different years, so it is a rough benchmark. The researchers read the gap as selection: among people on low incomes, those who take up coverage are sicker, just like our row.
    highlight oregon_marker_2 oregon_answer

Defenders of matching answer that richer records, years of claims and diagnoses, would close the gap. The way to find out is exactly this: run the method where a lottery already knows the answer.
    highlight oregon_marker_2

    quiz "A clinic offers free diet coaching. Researchers compare patients who joined with non-joiners who match them on age, weight and diagnoses. What would most bias the result?" intro "One new case."
        right: Their resolve is in no record, so the matched non-joiners are a poor stand-in for what the joiners would have done anyway.
        wrong: Matching takes care of what is on file. Which choice is about something no record holds?
        * Too few patients in the study
        + Joiners were already set on changing their habits
        * Both groups have the same average age

So can people who look alike on paper s (@animate {"box":{"x":90,"y":130,"w":560,"h":500}} duration 0.6@)tand in for a (@show axes x_label y_label yt0 yt2 yt4 yt6 xt1 xt2 curve_0 curve_1 lab_est lab_truth you@) lottery? Only if what is  (@draw takeaway_1@)on paper is everything that sent them into coverage.
    erase oregon oregon_answer

Matching admits where it has no one to compare w (@highlight curve_0 curve_1@)ith, and that changes whom the answer is about. A regression either drops that row too or fills it with a line.
    draw takeaway_2

Both lean on the same leap. Only a lottery, or some other st (@point at.ref you gesture circle@)roke of luck in who gets covered, can show whether it holds.
    draw takeaway_3

## Where next
